window.__hvBdRegister("plut-lives-1", {"key":"plut-lives-1","sections":[{"i":0,"j":32,"loc":"Life of Theseus, chs. 1–11","title":"Theseus: birth, youth, and the road to Athens","summary":"Plutarch explains why he pairs Theseus with Romulus and asks indulgence for treating fable as history. He traces Theseus's descent, the oracle given to Aegeus, and the tokens hidden under the rock at Troezen. Fired by the fame of his kinsman Heracles, the young Theseus refuses the safe sea route and travels to Athens by land. On the way he kills Periphetes, Sinis, the Crommyonian sow, Sciron, Cercyon and Procrustes, punishing each in the manner of his own crimes, though Plutarch notes the Megarian dissent about Sciron.","ideas":["Fable must be purified by reason to look like history","Theseus seeks danger in emulation of Heracles","The robbers suffer the same violence they inflicted","Conflicting local traditions are reported side by side"],"terms":[{"t":"Parallel Lives","d":"Plutarch's paired biographies of a Greek and a Roman, here Theseus and Romulus."},{"t":"gnōrismata (tokens)","d":"The sword and sandals Aegeus left under the rock so that his son could be recognized."}]},{"i":33,"j":65,"loc":"Life of Theseus, chs. 12–23","title":"Recognition at Athens and the Cretan voyage","summary":"Theseus is purified by the Phytalidae, survives Medea's poison plot when Aegeus recognizes his sword, and crushes the ambush of the sons of Pallas. After taming the Marathonian bull, he volunteers for the tribute of youths sent to Minos, and Plutarch sets the Minotaur story against rationalizing versions from Philochorus, Aristotle and Cleidemus. With Ariadne's help he prevails in Crete, though accounts of her fate differ widely. On the return he dances the Crane at Delos, forgets to change the sail, and Aegeus throws himself from the rock; festivals such as the Oschophoria and Pyanepsia are traced to these events.","ideas":["Aegeus recognizes his son by the sword at the banquet","Theseus volunteers to share the citizens' fate in the Cretan tribute","Rival versions of the Minotaur, Taurus and Ariadne","The forgotten white sail and the death of Aegeus"],"terms":[{"t":"Labyrinth","d":"Minos's maze holding the tribute youths; some Cretans called it merely a prison."},{"t":"Ship of Theseus","d":"The galley kept by the Athenians with replaced timbers, a philosophers' example of whether a thing stays the same."},{"t":"Oschophoria","d":"Athenian festival Plutarch traces to Theseus's return, with its crowned herald's staff and vine-branch procession."}]},{"i":66,"j":87,"loc":"Life of Theseus, chs. 24–29","title":"Founding Athens, Amazons, and other exploits","summary":"After Aegeus's death Theseus unites the scattered communities of Attica into one city, promises a government without a king, and divides citizens into nobles, farmers and craftsmen. He coins money, sets the boundary pillar on the Isthmus, and founds the Isthmian games. Plutarch then weighs accounts of his Amazon expedition and the Amazons' invasion of Athens, citing place-names and graves as evidence. He lists Theseus's less honourable marriages and his part in other heroic ventures, including the recovery of the dead from Thebes.","ideas":["Synoecism: one people of one city, named Athens","Theseus gives up absolute rule and leans toward the multitude","The Amazon war is attested by graves and local names","Theseus's many rapes and marriages are noted as discreditable"],"terms":[{"t":"synoikismos (union of settlements)","d":"Theseus's gathering of all Attica's residents into one city with a common council-chamber."},{"t":"Metoecia","d":"The Festival of Settlement he instituted, still held in Plutarch's day."}]},{"i":88,"j":104,"loc":"Life of Theseus, chs. 30–36","title":"Peirithoüs, Helen, exile and death","summary":"Theseus forms a sworn friendship with Peirithoüs and fights the Centaurs at his wedding. At fifty he joins in carrying off the child Helen, hides her at Aphidnae, and is imprisoned by the Molossian king Aïdoneus while helping Peirithoüs abduct his daughter. Meanwhile Menestheus stirs up Athens, the Dioscuri take Aphidnae and Aethra, and Theseus, freed by Heracles, cannot regain control. He withdraws to Scyros, where Lycomedes kills him; later the Athenians honour him as a demigod, and Cimon brings his bones home.","ideas":["Friendship of Theseus and Peirithoüs","The abduction of Helen brings war on Attica","Menestheus, first demagogue, turns the people against Theseus","Posthumous cult: Marathon apparition and return of the bones"],"terms":[{"t":"Anakes","d":"Title given to the Dioscuri at Athens, with Plutarch's etymologies for it."},{"t":"Theseum","d":"Theseus's tomb in Athens, a refuge for slaves and the powerless."}]},{"i":105,"j":120,"loc":"Life of Romulus, chs. 1–8","title":"Romulus: origins of Rome and the twins","summary":"Plutarch surveys conflicting accounts of how Rome got its name and of Romulus's parentage, including Promathion's tale of Tarchetius. He then gives the most widely credited version, from Diocles and Fabius Pictor: Amulius usurps Alba, the Vestal Ilia bears twins, and the exposed babies are suckled by a she-wolf and fed by a woodpecker. Reared by Faustulus, the brothers grow bold and attract followers; Remus is captured and brought before Numitor, who guesses his identity. Faustulus's trough reveals the truth, and the twins overthrow and kill Amulius, which Plutarch defends as plausible given Rome's divine fortune.","ideas":["Many rival etymologies and genealogies for Rome","Exposure, the she-wolf and the woodpecker","Recognition of Remus by Numitor","Fortune as a poet: marvels fit Rome's greatness"],"terms":[{"t":"lupa","d":"Latin for she-wolf and also a loose woman, which some say turned Acca Larentia into the wolf of story."},{"t":"manipulus","d":"A handful of hay on a pole carried by Romulus's companies, source of the word for maniples."}]},{"i":121,"j":140,"loc":"Life of Romulus, chs. 9–16","title":"Founding the city and seizing the Sabines","summary":"The twins restore Numitor and leave to found their own city, opening an asylum for fugitives. They quarrel over the site and settle it by augury, with Romulus seeing twelve vultures, perhaps falsely; Remus mocks the trench and is killed. Romulus founds the city with Etruscan rites, the mundus and the ploughed pomerium, on 21 April, and organizes legions, a senate of patricians, and the bond of patrons and clients. To gain wives and union with the Sabines he stages the rape of their daughters at the Consualia, then kills King Acron in single combat and dedicates the first spolia opima to Jupiter Feretrius.","ideas":["The augury of vultures and the death of Remus","Ritual founding with mundus and pomerium","Patricians, senate, patrons and clients","Rape of the Sabines as a means to blend peoples; first triumph"],"terms":[{"t":"pomerium","d":"The sacred boundary furrow traced by plough, broken only at the gates."},{"t":"patres conscripti","d":"The 'conscript fathers', the Roman senate's honorific title."},{"t":"spolia opima","d":"Spoils taken by a general who kills the enemy general with his own hand."}]},{"i":141,"j":165,"loc":"Life of Romulus, chs. 17–21","title":"Sabine war, reconciliation, and joint kingship","summary":"Rome defeats Fidenae, Crustumerium and Antemnae, then faces the Sabines under Tatius, to whom Tarpeia betrays the citadel and is crushed by their shields. In the battle in the valley Curtius nearly drowns in the marsh, and Romulus, struck by a stone, prays to Jupiter Stator to halt the rout. The Sabine wives rush between the armies and plead for peace, and Hersilia leads the appeals. A treaty makes Romulus and Tatius joint kings, merges the peoples as Quirites, and grants the women honours; Plutarch then explains shared festivals such as the Matronalia, Carmentalia and Lupercalia.","ideas":["Tarpeia's treachery and its punishment","Jupiter Stator halts the Roman flight","The Sabine women's intercession ends the war","Union of the two peoples and origins of the Lupercalia"],"terms":[{"t":"Quirites","d":"Name of all Roman citizens after the union, from the Sabine town of Tatius."},{"t":"Lupercalia","d":"February festival of purification with goat sacrifice and running Luperci, whose origin Plutarch debates."},{"t":"Comitium","d":"The meeting-place where the treaty was made, from the verb 'to come together'."}]},{"i":166,"j":189,"loc":"Life of Romulus, chs. 22–29","title":"Later reign, disappearance, and deification","summary":"Romulus is credited with religious institutions and laws on divorce and parricide. Tatius is killed after shielding kinsmen who murdered Laurentine envoys, and Romulus does not pursue his killers, until a plague forces punishment on both sides. Romulus wins wars against Cameria and Veii but grows autocratic, ignoring the senate. He vanishes in a storm, and Julius Proculus reports seeing him as the god Quirinus; Plutarch compares Greek vanishing tales and argues that only the purified soul, not the body, rises to the gods.","ideas":["Romulus's laws and the delayed justice for Tatius's death","Victory over Veii and the shift to monarchic arrogance","Mysterious disappearance and Proculus's vision of Quirinus","Plutarch: souls, not bodies, ascend from heroes to gods"],"terms":[{"t":"Quirinus","d":"The god Romulus became, linked by some to the spear (quiris)."},{"t":"lictores","d":"Staff-bearers who went before the king, with Plutarch's derivations of the word."},{"t":"Capratine Nones","d":"Festival of the day Romulus vanished, also linked to the stratagem of Philotis."}]},{"i":190,"j":196,"loc":"Comparison of Theseus and Romulus","title":"Comparison of Theseus and Romulus","summary":"Plutarch judges that Theseus sought great deeds by choice while Romulus acted from fear and necessity, and that Theseus's voluntary Cretan venture shows unequalled courage. Both strayed from true kingship, Theseus toward democracy and Romulus toward tyranny. Theseus's anger at his son is more excusable than Romulus's at his brother, but Romulus rose from the lowest beginnings, created a city from nothing, and saved his mother and grandfather. Theseus's many rapes admit no excuse and brought Athens wars, whereas Romulus's seizure of the Sabines created lasting union and stable marriage.","ideas":["Voluntary versus compelled courage","Kingship lost through laxity versus harshness","Theseus's negligence over the sail as near-parricide","Romulus's rape of the Sabines judged politically fruitful"],"terms":[{"t":"synkrisis (comparison)","d":"The closing formal weighing of the two paired lives."}]},{"i":197,"j":209,"loc":"Life of Lycurgus, chs. 1–5","title":"Lycurgus: lineage, regency, travels, and the senate","summary":"Plutarch notes that everything about Lycurgus is disputed, especially his date, and gives his descent from the Spartan kings. As regent he refuses his sister-in-law's offer to kill her baby and proclaims the newborn Charilaüs king, then travels to escape suspicion. In Crete he studies laws and sends the poet Thales to Sparta; in Ionia he collects Homer's poems; Egyptians claim he visited them. Recalled by the Spartans and encouraged by the Delphic oracle, he seizes the market-place with armed supporters and establishes a council of twenty-eight elders to steady the state between kings and people.","ideas":["Uncertain chronology of Lycurgus","Integrity as guardian of the infant king Charilaüs","Travels to Crete, Ionia and possibly Egypt","The senate as ballast between tyranny and democracy"],"terms":[{"t":"gerousia (Council of Elders)","d":"Twenty-eight senators who, with the two kings, made a body of thirty."},{"t":"prodikos","d":"Spartan term for the guardian of a fatherless king."}]},{"i":210,"j":228,"loc":"Life of Lycurgus, chs. 6–13","title":"The rhetra, land, iron money, common messes","summary":"Lycurgus obtains the Great Rhetra from Delphi, fixing tribes, the council and open-air assemblies where the people accept or reject proposals; later kings add a power to set aside distorted votes, and the ephors are created about 130 years after him. He redistributes the land into equal lots, replaces gold and silver with heavy iron money, and so drives out luxury and useless arts. He institutes the common messes, angering the rich, one of whom strikes out his eye. Plutarch describes mess customs and the unwritten rhetras on education, plain houses, and not fighting the same enemy too often.","ideas":["The Great Rhetra and the people's limited vote","Equal land lots and worthless iron currency","Common messes make wealth useless and unseen","Laws kept unwritten, embedded in education"],"terms":[{"t":"rhetra","d":"An ordinance of Lycurgus, regarded as coming from the god."},{"t":"phiditia","d":"The Spartan common messes, with Plutarch's possible derivations of the name."},{"t":"ephors","d":"Magistrates added later as a curb on the oligarchic element."}]},{"i":229,"j":241,"loc":"Life of Lycurgus, chs. 14–18","title":"Women, marriage, and the training of boys","summary":"Lycurgus treats education as the lawgiver's greatest task and begins with marriage and birth. Girls exercise and appear lightly clad at festivals, bachelors are shamed, and marriages are conducted secretly and soberly; husbands may share the begetting of children with worthy men, children being regarded as belonging to the state. Elders inspect newborns and expose weak infants. From seven, boys are trained in state companies under an eiren, kept hungry so they steal, flogged when caught, and questioned to sharpen their judgement, with lovers sharing their honour or disgrace.","ideas":["Physical training of girls and public praise and mockery","Secret marriages and shared begetting for good offspring","Inspection and exposure of infants","The agōgē: companies, hunger, theft and brief answers"],"terms":[{"t":"eiren","d":"A youth about twenty who commands a company of boys."},{"t":"paidonomos","d":"The citizen appointed overseer of the boys' training."},{"t":"Apothetae","d":"The chasm near Taÿgetus where deformed infants were exposed."}]},{"i":242,"j":267,"loc":"Life of Lycurgus, chs. 19–23","title":"Laconic speech, music, and conduct in war","summary":"Boys are taught a brief, pointed speech, and Plutarch gathers sayings of Lycurgus and later Spartans to show it. Their songs praise the brave dead and shame cowards, as in the three choirs of old men, young men and boys, and Plutarch argues that Spartans are both most musical and most warlike. In war discipline is relaxed, men groom their long hair, and they march to battle to the flute calmly and cheerfully. They spare fleeing enemies, and Plutarch weighs whether Lycurgus himself was a soldier or a man of peace linked with the Olympic truce.","ideas":["Brevity of speech as a discipline of thought","Spartan apophthegms","Music and valour joined","Calm, measured battle and mercy to the fleeing"],"terms":[{"t":"apophthegm","d":"A short, pointed saying, the characteristic Spartan form of speech."}]},{"i":268,"j":281,"loc":"Life of Lycurgus, chs. 24–31","title":"Spartan life, legacy, and Lycurgus's death","summary":"Adult Spartans live as in a camp, devoted to the city, with leisure because Helots work the land and money-making is despised. Senators over sixty are chosen by the volume of the assembly's shouting, burials are made simple, and foreign residents are expelled to keep out corrupting customs. Plutarch reports the krupteia and harsh treatment of Helots but refuses to attribute them to Lycurgus. Having bound Sparta by oath to keep his laws until he returned from Delphi, Lycurgus starves himself to death, and Sparta flourishes for five hundred years until Lysander brings in gold.","ideas":["Citizens belong to the city like bees to a hive","Election of elders by acclamation","Krupteia reported but denied to Lycurgus","Self-chosen death to make the oath binding; later decline"],"terms":[{"t":"krupteia (secret service)","d":"Young Spartans sent out to kill Helots, which Plutarch thinks a later practice."},{"t":"Helots","d":"The serf population that farmed Spartan land."},{"t":"Lycurgidae","d":"Days of the memorial assembly his friends instituted."}]},{"i":282,"j":296,"loc":"Life of Numa, chs. 1–7","title":"Numa chosen king after Romulus vanishes","summary":"Plutarch opens with the dispute over Numa's dates and his supposed link with Pythagoras. Romulus disappears in a storm, the patricians are suspected, and Romans and Sabines quarrel over a successor, ruling meanwhile by an interregnum of senators. They agree that the Romans will pick a Sabine, and choose Numa of Cures, a man of austere virtue who, after his wife Tatia's death, lived in sacred groves and was said to consort with the goddess Egeria. Plutarch weighs whether gods truly love such men. Numa at first refuses the throne as a lover of peace, but is persuaded by his father and Marcius, and is installed only after the augurs observe favourable birds.","ideas":["Interregnum: senators rule in turn for six hours each","Story of Egeria compared with other tales of divine lovers","Numa's speech refusing kingship over a warlike city","Augury on the Capitol ratifies his election"],"terms":[{"t":"interregnum","d":"The rotating rule of the senators between Romulus's death and Numa's election."},{"t":"Celeres","d":"Romulus's bodyguard of three hundred, which Numa disbanded."},{"t":"Flamen Quirinalis","d":"The priest of Romulus as Quirinus, added by Numa to those of Jupiter and Mars."}]},{"i":297,"j":310,"loc":"Life of Numa, chs. 8–12","title":"Taming Rome through religion; the priesthoods","summary":"Numa sets out to soften a feverish, warlike city with sacrifices, processions and fear of the gods. Plutarch lists parallels with Pythagoras: the cult of the silent Muse Tacita, the ban on images of the gods, bloodless offerings, and reports that Pythagoras was a Roman citizen. Numa founds the Pontifices, whose name Plutarch explains several ways, and the Vestal virgins, whose terms of service, privileges and burial alive for broken chastity he describes, along with the round temple of Vesta. He also regulates mourning and creates the Fetiales, priests who decide whether a war is just; their neglect is blamed for the Gallic sack.","ideas":["Religion used to tame a warlike people","No images of the gods in early Rome, as in Pythagorean teaching","Vestals: thirty years' service, burial alive for unchastity","Fetiales must declare a war just before it can begin"],"terms":[{"t":"Pontifex Maximus","d":"Chief priest who directs sacred rites and oversees the Vestals."},{"t":"Vestals","d":"Sacred virgins who guard the perpetual fire."},{"t":"Fetiales","d":"Priests who seek redress by parley and must judge a war just."}]},{"i":311,"j":319,"loc":"Life of Numa, chs. 13–17","title":"The heavenly shield, Pythagorean precepts, and the guilds","summary":"During a plague a bronze shield falls from heaven; Mamurius makes eleven copies and the Salii are founded to guard and dance with them. Numa builds the Regia and teaches his people to worship without distraction, with precepts resembling the hidden sayings of the Pythagoreans. His authority makes the people accept marvels such as the sudden banquet and his bargaining with Jupiter over the thunder charm. He builds temples to Faith and Terminus, fixes boundaries, distributes land to the poor to turn them to farming, and divides the people into trade guilds to dissolve the old split between Sabines and Romans.","ideas":["The ancilia and the leaping dance of the Salii","Worship as an undivided, unhurried act","Farming as a peace-potion for citizens","Trade guilds blur the Sabine–Roman division"],"terms":[{"t":"ancilia","d":"The sacred shields, one fallen from heaven and eleven copies."},{"t":"Terminus","d":"God of boundaries, honoured at first with bloodless sacrifice."},{"t":"pagi","d":"Rural districts into which Numa divided the land, with overseers."}]},{"i":320,"j":330,"loc":"Life of Numa, chs. 18–22","title":"Calendar reform, forty-three years of peace, death","summary":"Numa corrects the calendar with an intercalary month and reorders the months, putting January first; Plutarch discusses the month names and Janus as patron of civil order. The gates of Janus stay shut throughout his reign, and peace spreads to neighbouring cities, so that Rome shows Plato's saying about a king who is also a philosopher. Plutarch reports disputes about Numa's wives and children, then his death past eighty and his burial with his sacred books, which are later found and burned. The section ends with the violent fates of the kings who followed, especially Tullus Hostilius.","ideas":["Intercalary month Mercedinus and the reordering of months","Temple of Janus closed for forty-three years","Plato's philosopher-king realized in Numa","Sacred books buried with him, later burned by the senate"],"terms":[{"t":"Janus","d":"Two-faced god of civil order, whose temple doors stand open in war."}]},{"i":331,"j":349,"loc":"Comparison of Lycurgus and Numa","title":"Comparison: Lycurgus and Numa as lawgivers","summary":"Plutarch sets the two lawgivers side by side: Numa accepted a kingdom, Lycurgus gave one up; Lycurgus tightened a slack Sparta, Numa loosened a harsh Rome. Numa's order was popular and mixed, with mechanics and trades, while Lycurgus's was aristocratic and military; Numa gave slaves a taste of freedom at the Saturnalia but left wealth unchecked. They differ on marriage, the freedom of women and the age of brides. Plutarch judges Lycurgus superior in educating the young, which made his laws last five hundred years, whereas Numa's peace vanished at his death; yet Numa's winning a foreign city by persuasion alone remains divine.","ideas":["Tightening versus loosening the strings of the state","Numa's lack of public education undid his peace","Spartan versus Roman customs on wives and maidens","Numa ruled by persuasion, Lycurgus by arms"],"terms":[{"t":"Saturnalia","d":"Festival at which slaves feasted with their masters, ascribed to Numa."}]},{"i":350,"j":383,"loc":"Life of Solon, chs. 1–7","title":"Solon's family, trade, poetry and the sages","summary":"Solon is of Codrus's line, a kinsman and early friend of Peisistratus. With his father's estate reduced, he goes into trade, and Plutarch defends commerce as honourable, citing his verses that prize virtue over wealth. His poetry turns from amusement to political argument, and his philosophy is chiefly ethical. Plutarch tells of the tripod passed among the Seven Wise Men, Anacharsis comparing laws to spiders' webs, and Thales tricking Solon with false news of his son's death. Plutarch then argues that fear of loss is no reason to refuse wife and children; reason must fortify us against fortune.","ideas":["Commerce was honourable in Solon's time","The tripod given to the wisest","Laws as spiders' webs, Anacharsis's jibe","Reason, not avoidance of attachment, guards against grief"],"terms":[{"t":"Seven Wise Men","d":"The sages of Solon's era, mostly statesmen; only Thales speculated beyond practice."}]},{"i":384,"j":401,"loc":"Life of Solon, chs. 8–12","title":"Salamis won and the city purified","summary":"When Athens bans proposals to renew the war for Salamis, Solon feigns madness and recites his poem 'Salamis' in the market-place, and is given command. Two accounts of the capture follow: young men disguised as women ambush the Megarians, or Solon sacrifices to the local heroes and takes the city by ruse. Spartan arbiters award the island to Athens, aided, some say, by a verse Solon inserted into Homer. He also urges the war for Delphi, persuades the polluted Alcmaeonids to stand trial for the Cylonian killings, and with Epimenides of Crete purifies the city and paves the way for his laws.","ideas":["Feigned madness and the poem 'Salamis'","Disputed versions of the island's capture","Trial over the Cylonian pollution","Epimenides' purifications and his warning about Munychia"],"terms":[{"t":"Cylonian pollution","d":"The curse on Megacles' family for killing Cylon's followers who had taken sanctuary."}]},{"i":402,"j":440,"loc":"Life of Solon, chs. 13–16","title":"Debt crisis, refusal of tyranny, the Disburdenment","summary":"Athens is split into Hill, Plain and Shore parties, and the poor are enslaved for debt. Both sides turn to Solon, who is made archon and mediator; friends urge him to take a tyranny, but he refuses, saying it is a lovely place with no way down, and quotes the mockery of those who call him a fool. His first measure, the Seisactheia, cancels debts and forbids loans on the person, though some say it only lowered interest and changed the coinage. Friends who knew in advance profit by it, and Solon clears himself by remitting his own loans. Both rich and poor are at first displeased, but the people come round and give him full power to remake the laws.","ideas":["Debt slavery of the hectemoroi","Tyranny refused: no way down from it","Debts cancelled and record-stones removed","Solon pleases neither party but gains full legislative power"],"terms":[{"t":"Seisactheia (Disburdenment)","d":"Solon's cancelling of debts, named with a mild word."},{"t":"hectemoroi","d":"Poor tenants who paid a sixth of their produce to the rich."}]},{"i":441,"j":474,"loc":"Life of Solon, chs. 17–25","title":"Solon's laws and constitution","summary":"Solon repeals Draco's laws except on homicide and sorts citizens into four property classes, giving the lowest a place in the assembly and the popular courts, which his vague laws made powerful. He lets anyone prosecute on another's behalf, sets up the Council of Four Hundred beside the Areopagus, whose origin Plutarch questions, and disfranchises those neutral in faction. Plutarch reviews laws on heiresses, dowries, slander, wills, women's mourning, trades, illegitimate sons, sex crimes, water, planting, exports, citizenship and public meals. The laws are written on revolving axones for a hundred years, and Solon fixes the 'Old and New' day. Pestered with questions, he leaves on a ten-year journey.","ideas":["Four census classes; thetes sit as jurors","Two councils as a double anchor","Neutrals in civil strife lose citizenship","Laws made for the situation, not the reverse"],"terms":[{"t":"Pentakosiomedimnoi","d":"The top class, with yearly yield of five hundred measures."},{"t":"thetes","d":"The lowest class, barred from office but admitted to assembly and juries."},{"t":"axones / kyrbeis","d":"Revolving wooden tablets on which the laws were written."}]},{"i":475,"j":509,"loc":"Life of Solon, chs. 26–32","title":"Travels, Croesus, Peisistratus, and Solon's end","summary":"Solon studies with Egyptian priests, hears of Atlantis, and helps Philocyprus refound a city in Cyprus named Soli. At Sardis he tells Croesus that Tellus, then Cleobis and Bito, were happier than he, and that no one is happy until his end; later, on the pyre, Croesus calls on Solon and is spared by Cyrus. Back in Athens, factions revive under Lycurgus, Megacles and Peisistratus; Solon rebukes Thespis's fictions and opposes Peisistratus's bodyguard, then sets his arms outside his door when the tyranny is seized. Peisistratus honours him and keeps most of his laws; Solon abandons his Atlantis poem in old age, and sources differ on whether he lived long under the tyranny or less than two years.","ideas":["Call no man happy before his end","Croesus saved by remembering Solon","Solon alone resists Peisistratus's bodyguard and coup","Atlantis left unfinished by Solon and by Plato"],"terms":[]},{"i":510,"j":523,"loc":"Life of Publicola, chs. 1–8","title":"Valerius, the Tarquins' fall and the conspiracy","summary":"Publius Valerius, wealthy and eloquent, helps Brutus expel Tarquin after Lucretia's death, but is passed over for consul in favour of Collatinus. He withdraws, then is first to swear never to yield to the Tarquins and blocks the king's envoys from addressing the people. The envoys corrupt the Aquillii and Vitellii and Brutus's sons; the slave Vindicius overhears their oath and goes to Valerius, who seizes the letters. Brutus watches his sons beheaded, Valerius forces the trial of the other traitors, Collatinus withdraws, and Valerius becomes consul. Vindicius is made a citizen, and Tarquin's field becomes the Campus Martius and the Tiber island.","ideas":["Valerius passed over, then wins trust by his oath","Vindicius reveals the plot","Brutus executes his own sons","Origin of the island between the two bridges"],"terms":[{"t":"vindicta","d":"The term for full manumission, said to derive from Vindicius."}]},{"i":524,"j":539,"loc":"Life of Publicola, chs. 9–15","title":"Victory, popular reforms, and the Capitol temple","summary":"Brutus and Aruns kill each other in battle against the Tuscans; a voice from the grove declares Rome the winner, and Valerius triumphs and delivers a funeral oration for Brutus. When the people suspect him of kingly ambitions because of his grand house, he tears it down overnight and lowers the fasces before the assembly, earning the name Publicola. Before a colleague is chosen he fills the senate and passes laws allowing appeal to the people, punishing unconferred office, lightening taxes, permitting the killing of would-be tyrants, and placing the treasury in the temple of Saturn under quaestors. The terra-cotta chariot from Veii and Horatius's steady consecration of Jupiter's temple follow, with a digression on the later Capitols and Domitian's building mania.","ideas":["Voice in the grove decides the battle","House razed and fasces lowered to the people","Right of appeal and quaestors of the treasury","Horatius ignores false news of his son's death"],"terms":[{"t":"Publicola","d":"Surname meaning people-cherisher, given to Valerius."},{"t":"quaestors","d":"Two young treasurers chosen by the people to keep public money."}]},{"i":540,"j":555,"loc":"Life of Publicola, chs. 16–23","title":"War with Porsena, the Sabines, and death","summary":"Porsena besieges Rome for Tarquin; Horatius Cocles holds the bridge and swims the Tiber, and Mucius burns his own hand before the king, claiming three hundred others will follow. Publicola makes Porsena arbiter against Tarquin, who refuses, and peace is made with hostages; when Cloelia and the maidens escape, Publicola returns them, and Porsena leaves his stocked camp to Rome. Publicola wins over the Sabine Appius Clausus, who comes with five thousand families and founds the Claudian house, then defeats the Sabines through a coordinated ambush. He dies after his triumph, buried at public cost within the city and mourned a year by the women.","ideas":["Horatius Cocles at the bridge","Mucius Scaevola's burned hand","Cloelia and the returned hostages","Appius Clausus migrates to Rome"],"terms":[]},{"i":556,"j":567,"loc":"Comparison of Solon and Publicola","title":"Comparison: Solon and Publicola","summary":"Plutarch finds this pair unusual: Publicola fulfils Solon's verdict on happiness better than Tellus did, being honoured, victorious, lamented by the whole city and generous with honest wealth. Publicola in turn honoured Solon by copying his democratic measures, though he hated tyranny more fiercely. Solon's cancelling of debts was unique and ended sedition, and his career shone brighter at the start; Publicola's ended better, since his order lasted while Solon saw his undone. In war Publicola surpassed Solon, and his surrender of territory to Porsena is defended as yielding a part to save the whole.","ideas":["Publicola as the happiest man by Solon's own measure","Shared measures: appeal to the people, enlarged councils","Solon's work undone in his lifetime; Publicola's endured","Yielding small gains to secure greater ones"],"terms":[]},{"i":568,"j":592,"loc":"Life of Themistocles, chs. 1–7","title":"Themistocles' rise and the naval policy","summary":"Themistocles, of obscure family and a foreign mother, is an ambitious boy who composes mock speeches and scorns refined studies; Plutarch makes him a follower of Mnesiphilus's practical wisdom. His rivalry with Aristides begins in youth, and Miltiades' trophy at Marathon keeps him awake, since he foresees a greater war. He persuades Athens to spend the Laureium silver on triremes, turning the city to the sea, courts popularity, and has Aristides ostracized. Facing the Persians he buys off Epicydes, punishes the King's interpreter, reconciles the Greek cities, yields the command to Eurybiades, and uses Euboean money to hold the fleet at Artemisium.","ideas":["The trophy of Miltiades will not let him sleep","Silver of Laureium spent on a hundred triremes","Athenians made mariners instead of hoplites","Command ceded to Sparta for the sake of unity"],"terms":[{"t":"sophia","d":"Practical political cleverness taught by Mnesiphilus, said to descend from Solon."},{"t":"ostracism","d":"Removal of a citizen by vote, used against Aristides."}]},{"i":593,"j":612,"loc":"Life of Themistocles, chs. 8–12","title":"Artemisium, the evacuation, and the Sicinnus ruse","summary":"The fights at Artemisium teach the Greeks to close with the enemy. After Thermopylae Themistocles leaves messages urging the Ionians to desert, and, with the Peloponnesians walling the Isthmus, uses the serpent's disappearance and the 'wooden wall' oracle to persuade Athens to abandon the city to Athena and take to the ships; families go to Troezen and Xanthippus's dog swims to Salamis. He recalls the ostracized, and answers Eurybiades with 'Smite, but hear me.' When the Greeks again resolve to withdraw, he sends Sicinnus to tell Xerxes they are escaping, and Aristides brings word that the fleet is surrounded.","ideas":["The 'wooden wall' read as the fleet","Athens abandoned and its people put to sea","'Smite, but hear me'","Sicinnus's message traps the Greeks into fighting"],"terms":[]},{"i":613,"j":628,"loc":"Life of Themistocles, chs. 13–18","title":"The battle of Salamis and its honours","summary":"Xerxes watches from a golden throne while, by Phanias's account, three Persian prisoners are sacrificed before the battle at the seer's demand. Themistocles waits for the sea breeze, which swings the tall Persian ships broadside; Ariamenes is killed, and a light from Eleusis and visions of the Aeacidae are reported. After victory he tests Aristides with a plan to break the Hellespont bridge, then sends Arnaces to hasten Xerxes' retreat. The generals each vote him second, Sparta crowns him for wisdom, and Olympia gazes at him all day. A run of his sayings illustrates his love of honour.","ideas":["The sea breeze decides the fight in the narrows","Message to Xerxes speeds his retreat","Everyone's second choice for the prize of valour","Sayings: the plane-tree, the Seriphian, the festival day"],"terms":[]},{"i":629,"j":656,"loc":"Life of Themistocles, chs. 19–22","title":"Walls, the Piraeus, unpopularity and ostracism","summary":"Themistocles deceives Sparta while Athens rebuilds its walls, then fortifies the Piraeus and binds the city to the sea, strengthening the common people. His plan to burn the Greek fleet is judged by Aristides most advantageous and most unjust, and the people forbid it. He offends Sparta by defending the smaller cities in the Amphictyony and angers allies by extorting money; the poet Timocreon attacks him in songs. Boasting of his services and building a temple to Artemis Best Counsellor, he is ostracized, which Plutarch calls a relief for envy rather than a penalty.","ideas":["City fastened to the Piraeus, land to the sea","Sea power as the mother of democracy","Scheme to burn the fleet rejected","Ostracism as a soothing of envy"],"terms":[{"t":"Aristoboulé","d":"'Best Counsellor', the title he gave Artemis, implying his own counsel."}]},{"i":657,"j":683,"loc":"Life of Themistocles, chs. 23–32","title":"Flight to Persia, honours there, and death","summary":"Implicated by Pausanias's papers, Themistocles flees through Corcyra to Admetus of the Molossians, supplicating him with the king's son at the hearth, and then escapes by ship past Naxos to Asia. Hidden in a woman's curtained carriage he reaches the Persian court, does obeisance, and wins the King's favour; he asks a year to learn Persian, and is given three cities. A dream from the Mother of the Gods saves him from assassins. When the King demands he act against Greece, he drinks bull's blood or poison at Magnesia, aged sixty-five. Plutarch closes with his children, his tomb, and a descendant who was his friend.","ideas":["Supplication at the Molossian hearth","'I have Themistocles the Athenian'","Speech like a tapestry that must be unrolled","Suicide rather than war on Greece"],"terms":[{"t":"Chiliarch","d":"The Persian grand vizier who governs access to the King."}]},{"i":684,"j":702,"loc":"Life of Camillus, chs. 1–8","title":"Camillus, the siege of Veii, and the tithe","summary":"Plutarch notes that Camillus, five times dictator and called a Second Founder of Rome, was never consul. Camillus wins fame against the Aequians and Volscians, and as censor pushes unmarried men to marry widows. During the long siege of Veii the Alban lake rises, a captured Veientine seer and the Delphic oracle explain the sign, and Camillus as dictator takes the city by digging mines up into the temple of Juno. His triumph with four white horses, his opposition to moving half the people to Veii, and his forgotten vow of a tenth of the spoil to Apollo make him unpopular; the Roman women give their gold for the offering to Delphi.","ideas":["Camillus held great commands but never the consulship, because of plebeian hostility to it","The Alban lake prodigy and the oracle that Veii falls only when the lake's water is kept from the sea","Camillus prays that any retribution for Veii's fall land on himself, then stumbles","Plutarch advises caution between credulity and disbelief about speaking statues"],"terms":[{"t":"dictator","d":"A single magistrate with absolute authority, appointed by the Romans in emergencies in place of the regular officials."},{"t":"military tribunes with consular power","d":"Boards of up to six commanders that the people elected in place of two consuls in Camillus's time."},{"t":"tithe (dekatē)","d":"The tenth of the Veii booty that Camillus had vowed to the Delphian god and that the soldiers later had to repay."}]},{"i":703,"j":729,"loc":"Life of Camillus, chs. 9–21","title":"The Falerian schoolmaster, exile, and the Allia","summary":"Camillus besieges Falerii and sends back a schoolmaster who tried to betray the city's boys, and the Falerians, won over by his justice, surrender. Accused of keeping Veii spoil, he goes into exile praying that Rome will soon need him. The Gauls, drawn to Italy by wine and led there by the Tuscan Arron, besiege Clusium; when the Roman envoy Fabius fights them in breach of an envoy's role and Rome refuses to hand him over, Brennus marches on Rome and routs a poorly led army at the Allia. Plutarch digresses on lucky and unlucky days, the Vestal fire, and the old senators who await death in the Forum.","ideas":["Camillus holds that even war has laws good men respect","Brennus claims the law of the stronger, the same law Rome itself follows","Rome's defeat is blamed on neglected rites, divided command, and its treatment of Camillus","Fire, the most active force in nature, kept by the Vestals (some say) as an image of the force that orders the universe"],"terms":[{"t":"Fetiales","d":"Priests founded by Numa to guard peace and judge whether a war could rightly be made."},{"t":"dies Alliensis","d":"The day of the Allia defeat, held by the Romans to be one of the unluckiest of the year."},{"t":"Vestal virgins","d":"Priestesses who keep the undying fire and the hidden sacred objects of Rome."}]},{"i":730,"j":742,"loc":"Life of Camillus, chs. 22–30","title":"Gauls take Rome; Camillus delivers it","summary":"Brennus enters the empty city, his men kill the seated elders after Papirius strikes a Gaul, and they burn Rome while the Capitol holds out. In exile at Ardea, Camillus leads a night attack that destroys a drunken Gallic force, but he refuses to command the Roman refugees until those on the Capitol elect him; Pontius Cominius climbs the cliff and gets him named dictator. The Gauls follow Pontius's track up the rock, but the sacred geese wake the garrison and Manlius throws them back. When the Romans are weighing out a thousand pounds of gold and Brennus throws his sword on the scale, Camillus arrives, annuls the bargain, and routs the Gauls, then triumphs and purifies the city.","ideas":["Camillus's respect for legal authority even in exile","The geese of Juno save the Capitol","'Woe to the vanquished' and Camillus's answer that Rome is ransomed with iron, not gold","Aristotle and Heracleides Ponticus heard reports of the city's capture"],"terms":[{"t":"Capitol","d":"Rome's fortified hill and citadel, the last part of the city held against the Gauls."}]},{"i":743,"j":752,"loc":"Life of Camillus, chs. 31–35","title":"Rebuilding Rome and the war with the Latins","summary":"Faced with the ruins, the people again want to move to Veii, and agitators accuse Camillus of wanting to be called Rome's founder. In the Senate a centurion's chance order to plant the standard there is taken as a divine sign, and the city is rebuilt quickly but in a tangle of streets; Romulus's augural staff is found unburnt. As dictator a third time, Camillus faces Aequians, Volscians, Latins and Tuscans: Plutarch gives the tale of the maid Tutula or Philotis behind the Capratine Nones, then the usual account in which Camillus burns the Latin palisades with fire-darts and wind. He then retakes Sutrium the same day it fell to the Tuscans.","ideas":["A chance word in the street settles the vote to stay in Rome","The finding of Romulus's lituus is read as a sign of lasting safety","Two explanations of the Capratine Nones festival","Sutrium taken twice in a single day"],"terms":[{"t":"lituus","d":"The curved augur's staff used to mark out quarters of the sky in taking bird omens."},{"t":"Capratine Nones","d":"A Roman festival on the Nones of July, explained by a fig-tree story or by the vanishing of Romulus."}]},{"i":753,"j":765,"loc":"Life of Camillus, chs. 36–43","title":"Manlius, the plebeian consulship, and Camillus's death","summary":"Marcus Manlius Capitolinus, jealous of Camillus, courts debtors in an attempt at tyranny; Camillus moves his trial out of sight of the Capitol and Manlius is thrown from the rock. Though old and sick, Camillus is forced to command, rescues the army after his colleague Lucius Furius's rash defeat, then honours Furius and pardons the Tusculans. He resigns a dictatorship rather than fight the people over Licinius Stolo's laws, but at almost eighty he defeats a new Gallic host with iron helmets and thrusting javelins. In a final civil struggle he vows a temple to Concord, the Senate yields a plebeian consul, and he dies in the next year's plague.","ideas":["Manlius's trial is moved so the judges cannot see the scene of his great deed","Camillus restores the honour of the colleague who disobeyed him","Iron helmets and spear-like javelins defeat the Gallic slashing swords","The temple of Concord marks the settlement that gave a plebeian consul"],"terms":[{"t":"Licinius Stolo's law","d":"A measure forbidding anyone to own more than five hundred acres, which Stolo himself was later punished for breaking."},{"t":"Concord (Concordia)","d":"The goddess to whom Camillus vowed and the people built a temple facing the Forum after the reconciliation."}]},{"i":766,"j":792,"loc":"Life of Pericles, chs. 1–9","title":"Why study virtue; Pericles' birth and teachers","summary":"Plutarch opens by arguing that the mind should be turned to virtuous deeds, which stir the wish to imitate them, unlike admired crafts whose makers we may despise; he pairs Pericles with Fabius Maximus for their gentleness and endurance of popular folly. Pericles, of the family of Cleisthenes, has an oddly long head mocked by the comic poets. He learns from Damon, Zeno, and above all Anaxagoras, who gives him his dignity, freedom from superstition, and lofty style, earning him the name Olympian. Fearing ostracism, he enters politics on the side of the many against Cimon, keeps himself aloof, and begins to win the people with public pay.","ideas":["Virtue's deeds rouse emulation; the products of crafts do not","Anaxagoras's teaching of Mind (Nous) as ordering cause shapes Pericles' character and speech","The seer and the naturalist can both be right: one gives the meaning, the other the cause","Pericles uses public moneys to outbid Cimon's private generosity"],"terms":[{"t":"Nous (Mind)","d":"Anaxagoras's principle that Mind, not chance or necessity, set the universe in order."},{"t":"deisidaimonia (superstition)","d":"Fear arising from ignorance of the causes of celestial events, which natural philosophy removes."},{"t":"Olympian","d":"Pericles' surname, which Plutarch traces chiefly to the thunder of his oratory."}]},{"i":793,"j":822,"loc":"Life of Pericles, chs. 10–17","title":"Pericles' rivals and the building of Athens","summary":"Pericles has Cimon recalled after Tanagra and, Plutarch argues, did not murder Ephialtes as Idomeneus alleged. Thucydides son of Melesias organizes the aristocrats against him, while Pericles pleases the people with festivals, paid fleets, and colonies. His great building programme, paid from the allies' tribute, is attacked as an outrage on Greece; he replies that Athens owes the allies only defence, and the works employ every trade. The Parthenon, Odeum, and Propylaea rise with astonishing speed under Pheidias; after Thucydides is ostracized, Pericles rules for fifteen years as an incorruptible, almost kingly statesman, careful with his own estate and attentive to Anaxagoras.","ideas":["The building programme as both lasting glory and work for the whole city","Works made quickly yet fit for all time, never looking old","After ostracizing Thucydides, Pericles leads the people like a physician, by their hopes and fears","A statesman needs wealth as a philosopher does not"],"terms":[{"t":"ostracism","d":"A vote that banished a leading citizen for ten years, used by Pericles to remove Thucydides."},{"t":"Delian funds","d":"The allies' common treasury, moved from Delos to Athens and spent in part on the city's temples."}]},{"i":823,"j":856,"loc":"Life of Pericles, chs. 18–29","title":"Cautious general, Aspasia, and the Samian War","summary":"Pericles is known for caution: he warns Tolmides before his fatal Boeotian campaign, secures the Chersonese, sails round the Peloponnese and into the Euxine, but restrains Athenian dreams of Egypt, Sicily, and Carthage. He bribes the Spartan adviser Cleandridas to lead Pleistoanax home and subdues Euboea. Plutarch digresses on Aspasia of Miletus, her reputed political wisdom and her place in Pericles' house. The Samian War follows: Pericles beats the Samians, suffers a setback from Melissus, then besieges and takes the city in nine months, rejecting Duris's tale of atrocities; finally, as war with the Peloponnesians nears, his handling of Corcyra and the Megarians angers Corinth and Sparta.","ideas":["'Time' as the wisest counsellor, Pericles' warning to Tolmides","Restraining Athens to defend what it already held","The 'white day' of the bean lottery at Samos","Elpinice's rebuke over the war against a kindred city"],"terms":[{"t":"promanteia","d":"The privilege of consulting the Delphic oracle first, which Pericles took from Sparta for Athens."},{"t":"Aspasia","d":"Milesian woman, teacher of rhetoric by repute, whom Pericles took into his house and loved deeply."}]},{"i":857,"j":889,"loc":"Life of Pericles, chs. 30–39","title":"The Megarian decree, plague, and Pericles' death","summary":"Pericles refuses to rescind the decree against Megara, whether from principle or pride, and so is held responsible for the war. Plutarch reports the worst charge: that attacks on Pheidias, Aspasia, and Anaxagoras made Pericles fear trial and fan the war. During the invasion he keeps the people within the walls like a helmsman in a storm, endures abuse from Cleon and the comic poets, and calms a steersman during an eclipse with his cloak. The plague turns the people against him; they fine and depose him, he loses his sons Xanthippus and Paralus, then is recalled and dies of a slow sickness. His proudest claim was that no Athenian ever wore mourning because of him, and Plutarch closes by praising his gentleness and freedom from envy.","ideas":["The ambiguous motives behind the Megarian decree","Pericles as helmsman ignoring the panicking passengers","The eclipse explained by the cloak held before the steersman's eyes","'Olympian' fits a power used without envy or rage"],"terms":[{"t":"Megarian decree","d":"The Athenian ban on the Megarians, whose repeal Pericles refused and which Plutarch treats as the main cause of the war."},{"t":"Cylonian pollution","d":"An ancient curse on Pericles' maternal family that Sparta demanded Athens expel."}]},{"i":890,"j":904,"loc":"Life of Fabius Maximus, chs. 1–7","title":"Fabius named dictator and his strategy of delay","summary":"Fabius, called Verrucosus and Ovicula, seems sluggish as a child but proves firm and prudent. After Hannibal's victories at the Trebia and Lake Trasimene, where the reckless Flaminius falls, Rome makes Fabius dictator. He begins by restoring the rites to the gods, then shadows Hannibal from the hills, refusing battle to wear him down, despite being mocked as Hannibal's paedagogue. When Hannibal is trapped at Casilinum, he escapes by driving cattle with burning torches on their horns at the passes. Fabius is blamed further when Hannibal spares his fields and when he sells his own land to ransom prisoners the Senate would not pay for.","ideas":["Fabius's slowness is steadiness and his caution is prudence","Wearing down Hannibal rather than risking battle","Hannibal's torch-bearing cattle trick at the passes","Fabius ransoms Roman captives from his own estate"],"terms":[{"t":"Sibylline books","d":"Oracular books consulted in the crisis, whose findings were kept secret."},{"t":"Master of Horse","d":"The dictator's deputy, here the rash Marcus Minucius."}]},{"i":905,"j":920,"loc":"Life of Fabius Maximus, chs. 8–16","title":"Minucius rescued; the disaster of Cannae","summary":"While Fabius is in Rome, Minucius wins a small success and the tribune Metilius has him made equal in command to the dictator. Fabius bears the slight calmly, splits the army, and when Hannibal ambushes Minucius, marches to save him; Minucius then admits his error, calls Fabius Father, and places himself under him. After Fabius lays down office, the consul Terentius Varro raises a huge army and seeks battle, while Fabius urges his colleague Paulus Aemilius to resist. At Cannae Hannibal's wind, dust, and crescent formation envelop the Romans, Varro flees, and Paulus dies refusing rescue, sending word to Fabius that he kept faith.","ideas":["A good man cannot truly be insulted or dishonoured","Fabius saves the colleague who had undermined him","Hannibal's crescent at Cannae encircles the Roman army","Paulus's last message to Fabius"],"terms":[{"t":"Patrons (patroni)","d":"The name freedmen give their former masters, used by Minucius's soldiers for Fabius's men."}]},{"i":921,"j":942,"loc":"Life of Fabius Maximus, chs. 17–27","title":"Rome's shield: Tarentum, Scipio, and Fabius's death","summary":"Hannibal fails to march on Rome after Cannae, and Fabius's caution now looks like divine foresight; he steadies the city, limits mourning, and joins in welcoming Varro back. With Marcellus as Rome's sword and Fabius as its shield, Hannibal is worn down; Fabius treats wavering soldiers with kindness, recovers Tarentum through a lovesick Bruttian commander, but stains the capture with a massacre. He yields to his son's authority as consul and bears his death calmly. In old age he opposes Scipio's invasion of Africa out of caution and, Plutarch judges, rivalry, and he dies before Hannibal's defeat, buried with each citizen's smallest coin.","ideas":["Fabius the buckler and Marcellus the sword","Gentle discipline reclaims soldiers as farmers tame wild trees","The capture and plunder of Tarentum","Fabius's opposition to Scipio, partly prudence and partly jealousy"],"terms":[{"t":"delaying strategy","d":"Fabius's policy of avoiding pitched battle so that Hannibal would wear himself out."}]},{"i":943,"j":948,"loc":"Comparison of Pericles and Fabius Maximus","title":"Comparison of Pericles and Fabius Maximus","summary":"Plutarch compares the two as generals: Pericles led Athens at the height of its power, while Fabius lifted Rome out of disaster, though restraining a proud people is harder than guiding a humbled one. Pericles won nine trophies and rightly foresaw Athens' decline, while Fabius saved Minucius but was tricked by the oxen and wrongly opposed Scipio's success. As statesmen, Fabius was gentler to rivals than Pericles was to Cimon and Thucydides, but Pericles had more power to restrain others. Both were free of greed, and Pericles' buildings surpass everything Rome built before the Caesars.","ideas":["Leading a prosperous people versus rescuing a defeated one","Pericles' foresight against Fabius's error over Scipio","Fabius's gentleness to rivals against Pericles' ostracisms","Both men incorruptible"],"terms":[{"t":"synkrisis (comparison)","d":"The closing set-piece in which Plutarch weighs the paired Greek and Roman lives against each other."}]}]});